<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="section_covers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Covers</title>
<p>
  
</p>

<subsection>
    <title>Fundamental Groups of Topological Groups</title>

    Here we assume some familiarity with algebraic topology and quickly review some of the relevant definitions and results. 

    <definition xml:id="definition-loops">
        <title>Loops</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>(X,x_0)</m> is a pointed topological space, then a <term>based loop</term> is a map 
                <me> c:[0,1]\to X </me> such that <m>c(0) = c(1) = x_0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>  We may define the <term>concatenation</term> of two based loops <m>c_1, c_2:[0,1]\to X</m> by
    <me> (c_1 * c_2)(t) = \begin{cases} c_1(2t) \amp 0\le t \le 1/2 \\ c_2(2t-1) \amp 1/2 \le t \le 1 \end{cases} </me>
    Concatenation defines a group operation on based homotopy classes of loops.  The set of based loops up to homotopy forms a group
    <me> \pi_1(X,x_0) = \{ [c] : c \text{ is a based loop in } X \text{ based at } x_0 \} </me> called the <term>fundamental group</term> of <m>(X,x_0)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Now suppose that <m>(G,e)</m> is a topological group with <m>e</m> as the identity element.  Then we may also define the pointwise multiplication of two based loops <m>c_1, c_2:[0,1]\to G</m> is given by
    <me> (c_1 \cdot c_2)(t) = c_1(t) \cdot c_2(t) </me>
    where the product on the right-hand side is the group operation in <m>G</m>.  Amazingly, concatenation and pointwise multiplication of loops are compatible!
    </p>    


        <proposition xml:id="proposition-compatibility_of_loop_operations">
        <title>Compatibility of Loop Operations</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4</m> are loops based at the identity of a topological group <m>G</m>, then 
                <m>(c_1 * c_2) \cdot (c_3 * c_4) = (c_1 \cdot c_3) * (c_2 \cdot c_4)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

        <proposition xml:id="proposition-concatenation_and_pointwise_multiplication_are_homotopic">
        <title>Concatenation and Pointwise Multiplication are Homotopic</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>c_1,c_4</m> are loops based at the identity of a topological group <m>G</m>, then 
                <m>[c_1 \cdot c_4] = [c_1 * c_4]</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

     <proposition xml:id="proposition-fundamental_group_of_a_topological_group_is_abelian">
        <title>The Fundamental Group of a Topological Group is Abelian</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>G</m> is a topological group, then its fundamental group <m>\pi_1(G,e)</m> is abelian.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

</subsection>

<subsection>
    <title>Covering Spaces</title>







    <definition xml:id="definition-covering_space">
        <title>Covering Space</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>X</m> is a topological space, then a <term>covering space</term> and <term>covering map</term> is a pair <m>(\tilde{X}, \pi)</m> where <m>\pi: \tilde{X} \to X</m> is a continuous surjective map such that every point in <m>X</m> has a neighborhood <m>U</m> for which <m>\pi^{-1}(U)</m> is a disjoint union of open sets, each of which is mapped homeomorphically onto <m>U</m> by <m>\pi</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>


    <identity xml:id="blackbox_theorem-lifting_of_maps">
        <title>Lifting of Maps</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>X</m> is a topological space, <m>\pi: \tilde{X} \to X</m> a covering map, <m>Y</m> is a path-connected, locally path-connected space, and <m>f:Y\to X</m> is continuous map, then there exists a lift <m>\tilde{f}: Y \to \tilde{X}</m> such that <m>f = \pi \circ \tilde{f}</m> if and only if <me>f_* (\pi_1(Y)) \subset \pi_*(\pi_1(\tilde{X})).</me>
            </p>
        <image width="30%" xml:id="diagram-lifting_of_maps">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzcd}
                \amp \tilde{X} \arrow[d,"\pi"] \\
                Y \arrow[ru,"\tilde{f}",dashrightarrow,] \arrow[r, "f"] \amp B \\
                \end{tikzcd}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
        </statement>
    </identity>



     <proposition xml:id="proposition-cover_of_a_topological_group_is_a_topological_group">
        <title>Cover of a Topological Group is a Topological Group</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>G</m> is a connected topological group, <m>p:G'\to G</m> a connected covering space, and <m>e'\in p^{-1}(e)</m> for some <m>e\in G</m>, then <m>G'</m> admits a unique topological group structure such that <m>p</m> is a group homomorphism and <m>e'</m> is the identity element.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

     <proposition xml:id="proposition-cover_of_a_lie_group_is_a_topological_group">
        <title>Cover of a Lie Group is a Lie Group</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>p:G'\to G</m> is a covering map of topological groups, and <m>G</m> is a connected Lie group, then <m>G'</m> admits a unique Lie group structure such that <m>p</m> is a Lie group homomorphism.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

</subsection>



<subsection>
    <title>Simply Connected Covers</title>

    <definition xml:id="definition-simply_connected_cover">
        <title>Simply Connected Cover</title>   
        <statement>
            <p>
                A simply connected covering space of a topological space <m>X</m> is a covering space <m>\tilde{X}</m> such that <m>\pi_1(\tilde{X})</m> is trivial.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <identity xml:id="theorem-simply_connected_covering_spaces">
        <title>Existence of Simply Connected Covering Spaces</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>X</m> is a path connected, locally path connected, and semilocally simply connected, then there exists a simply connected covering space <m>\tilde{X}</m> and a covering map <m>\pi: \tilde{X} \to X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </identity>

    <p> 
        By the lifting property <xref ref="blackbox_theorem-lifting_of_maps"/> simply connected covering spaces cover all other covers.  This is a <q>universal</q> property and hence are simetimes called <term>universal covers</term>.  As a consequence, simply connected covering spaces are unique up to homeomorphism. 
    </p>


    <proposition xml:id="theorem-lifting_of_lie_algebra_homomorphisms">
        <title>Lifting of Lie Algebra Homomorphisms</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>G</m> is a simply connected Lie group, <m>G'</m> is a Lie group, and <m>T:L(G) \to L(G')</m> is a Lie algebra homomorphism, then there exists a unique Lie group homomorphism <m>\tilde{T}: G \to G'</m> such that <m>d\tilde{T} = T</m>.
            </p>
        <image width="40%" xml:id="diagram-of_lie_algebra_homomorphisms">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzcd}
                G   \arrow[r,"\tilde{T}",dashrightarrow] \amp G' \\
                L(G) \arrow[u,"\exp"] \arrow[r, "T"] \amp L(G')\arrow[u,"\exp"] \\
                \end{tikzcd}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
        </statement>
    </proposition>


    <proposition xml:id="proposition-covering_space_lie_group">
        <title>Covering Space of a Lie Group</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>G</m> is a connected Lie group, then there exists a simply connected Lie group <m>\tilde{G}</m> and a covering map <m>\pi: \tilde{G} \to G</m> which is also a Lie group homomorphism.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>


        <proposition xml:id="proposition-equivalence_of_categories">
        <title>Equivalence of Categories</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The category of simply connected Lie groups and Lie group homomorphisms is equivalent to the category of finite dimensional real Lie algebras and Lie algebra homomorphisms. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>


    
</subsection>

</section>